{
 "id": "haul-on-the-bowline",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Haul on the Bowline",
 "description": "A pulling shanty as John Short of Watchet sang it to Cecil Sharp, for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano is the shantyman, who sings the chorus first as his call and each verse's first line alone, and everyone answers \"Haul on the bowline, the bowline haul\" in harmony, landing together on the last \"haul\", where Sharp marks the pull. It ends with the whole chorus sung by everyone.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "intermediate",
 "genre": [
  "sea shanty",
  "folk",
  "traditional"
 ],
 "style": "free",
 "tempo": 80,
 "key": "D",
 "timeSignature": "4/4",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "traditional sea shanty (as sung by John Short, Watchet; printed 1914)",
  "tune": "traditional sea shanty (as sung by John Short, Watchet; printed 1914)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella, on Cecil Sharp's harmony"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: traditional, printed 1914",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional, printed 1914",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella on the harmony of Cecil Sharp's piano accompaniment (public domain: printed 1914, Sharp died 1924); the tune as printed, with breaths written in for beginners. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): the tune's own rhythm as Sharp prints it: \"dar-\" in \"my dar-ling,\" (twice) starts a turn of three triplet semiquavers (B, C sharp, B) before its A, so its first note is 0.13 seconds though the syllable lasts a beat, and \"ling,\" follows as a semiquaver; and \"mi-zen\" is two semiquavers.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "tune, words and harmony from Cecil J. Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (London: Simpkin, Marshall, Hamilton, Kent & Co.; Schott & Co.; Taunton: Barnicott & Pearce, 1914), No. XXXVII, \"Haul on the Bow-line\", pp. 42 to 43, with Sharp's note on p. 71 (\"Sung by Mr. John Short, at Watchet. See also Tozer (No. 18), Bullen (No. 37), Bradford and Fagge (No. 1), and Miss Smith (p. 13).\"), in C major as printed and written here a tone up in D; every word and section as printed (the solo introduction, two verses each with its chorus, and the full chorus); the pull on Sharp's arrow-head over each chorus's last \"haul\" (his note on p. 32: \"In this and the following chanteys arrow-heads are placed immediately over the notes upon which the pulls are made\"). Read from the scan at archive.org/details/englishfolkchant00shar"
 },
